WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:06.880
This episode was made possible by generous supporters on Patreon.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:08.180
Hey Crazies.

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:11.660
In the previous video, we learned what 
electric charge really was:

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:15.140
A measure of how much something can
affect the electric field.

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:17.060
So the next logical question is:

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:19.400
Does the magnetic field work the same way?

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:22.660
Is there some kind of magnetic charge that
affects the magnetic field?

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:25.760
That would be nice, but it’s a little more
complicated than that.

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:29.840
No, the magnetic field is affected by the
same thing as the electric field:

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:31.420
electric charge.

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:34.280
This proton has a positive electric charge.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:37.740
If it’s sitting still, then only the electric field is affected.

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:41.480
But if it moves, it can also affect the magnetic field.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:46.060
The orange Xs and dots represent direction
into and out of your screen

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:49.900
because that’s what a simple arrow would
look like in each of those cases.

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:53.480
We can see the moving proton affects both
fields.

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:57.360
Historically though, we don’t do experiments
with single charged particles.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:01.340
So, while this diagram is accurate,
it’s not very practical.

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:06.180
We usually have a bunch of charges moving
together in something we call a current.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:07.440
To the timeline!

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:12.500
In 1819, Hans Christian Ørsted noticed that
magnetic compasses deflected

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:14.360
near a current-carrying wire.

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:15.600
Kind of like this.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:16.380
On.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:17.060
Off.

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:17.920
On.

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:18.660
Off.

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:19.400
On.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:20.180
Off.

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:25.780
Then in 1820, Jean-Baptiste Biot and Félix
Savart found a simple pattern for this.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:29.800
But then Pierre-Simon Laplace almost immediately generalized it

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:32.220
because, you know, mathematicians are like that.

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:33.260
Side Note!

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:34.980
Laplace was a BAMF!

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:35.700
Seriously!

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:38.100
He was, like, 70 by the time he did this.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:42.480
To honor Laplace for his hard work, we named
his law: Laplace’s Law.

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:43.460
Just kidding!

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:45.160
It’s called the Biot-Savart Law.

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:46.100
What?!?!?!

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:47.660
His name isn’t even on it?!

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:48.800
Yeah, I know.

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:53.200
It totally sucks, but, in all fairness,
the man has plenty of stuff named after him.

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:54.860
He doesn’t really need it.

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:59.440
Even electrodynamics, the topic of this series,
has a Laplace’s equation.

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:01.300
He did a lot of stuff in his life.

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:02.400
End of side note!

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:06.420
The point is magnetism appears when charge moves.

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:08.240
It doesn’t matter if it’s a single charge

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:10.940
or a whole bunch of charges moving through a long wire.

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:13.420
It doesn’t even matter how that wire is shaped.

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:16.340
Moving charge affects the magnetic field.

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:20.780
Sure, that’s true for electromagnets, but
that doesn’t really explain this.

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:22.480
Are you really going to make me go there?

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:24.740
Yes, the crazies are going to like it.

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:27.360
Ok, I can do this.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:30.980
We have what seem to be two different kinds of magnets.

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:34.020
An electromagnet and what we call a permanent magnet.

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:38.980
But, in the end, we’ll see both types of
magnets are really caused by the same thing.

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:40.340
Let’s start with the names.

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:43.800
An electromagnet is magnet created by electricity.

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:47.000
There isn’t any electric current running
through a magnet like this though,

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:49.260
so it seemed like it should go by a different name.

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:52.620
We went with permanent magnet, because we
thought they lasted forever.

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:56.900
Over time though, they can lose their magnetism,
especially if they get hot.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:01.320
But, left to their own devices, that process
could easily take thousands of years.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:04.740
Compared to a human life span, that still
seems like forever.

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:07.060
Anyway, it’s the name we’re stuck with.

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:08.600
Now for the basic properties.

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:13.100
Based on the shape of the magnetic field,
we notice there are two opposite sources.

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:17.000
We call these sources poles and label them
north and south.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:20.340
Why we use those labels has to do with the
Earth’s magnetic field,

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:22.240
but that’s a topic for another day.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:26.800
All permanent magnets have at least one north
pole and one south pole.

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:30.000
Even electromagnets have poles if they’re
shaped certain ways.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.960
Sometimes magnets have more than one set of poles,

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:34.980
but they always come in pairs.

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:36.240
Always!

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:40.200
It’s a behavior summed up pretty well with
Gauss’s law for magnetism.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:44.000
So how is a piece of material magnetic if
there’s no electric current?

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:46.080
Quantum mechanics.

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:47.620
Hold onto your butts.

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:51.560
To understand how something like this can
be a magnet, we need to look closer...

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:52.760
...a lot closer.

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:54.720
Super zoom!

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:57.840
This is what a chunk of iron looks like on
a molecular level.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:00.560
Yet, we still don’t see any moving charges.

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:03.200
For that, we have to zoom in one more step.

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:07.220
This cloud of negative charge is made of 26 electrons.

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:10.540
Each of those electrons is in something called an orbital.

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:15.560
Those come in a variety of shapes, each with a set of available properties.

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:17.480
But we need to be careful.

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:20.540
Quantum particles can have all sorts of properties:

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:25.140
position, energy, linear momentum, angular momentum.

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:26.740
All the properties!

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:30.880
The property the electrons have in these orbitals
is angular momentum.

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:36.560
In fact, we can measure both the total amount
and the orientation, at least along one direction.

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:40.660
Just having that property is enough to make
it a tiny magnet.

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:45.800
Any electron with a non-zero angular momentum
will act like a tiny magnet.

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:51.160
Unfortunately, that doesn’t necessarily
turn the entire iron atom into a magnet

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:54.280
because electrons tend to pair up in opposite directions.

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:57.100
There 26 electrons in an iron atom.

00:04:57.100 --> 00:04:59.460
For each one that’s in a clockwise orbital,

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:02.440
there’s another one that’s in a counterclockwise orbital.

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:04.180
They cancel each other out,

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:07.060
so this level of existence isn’t deep enough.

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:08.960
We need to zoom in even closer.

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:14.240
This single electron has an inherent property
called spin angular momentum.

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:16.660
That’s something I’ve talked a lot about in this video.

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:21.480
It’s not really a motion, but it is momentum
and that’s enough for magnetism.

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:26.500
When you zoom back out to the atomic level,
most of those electrons still pair up and cancel,

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:28.200
except four of them.

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:32.060
Because like charges repel, they get as far
from each other as possible

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:35.220
and line up in the same direction, at least
in iron.

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:38.080
The more of those loner electrons an atom
has lined up,

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:40.040
the more magnetic it’s going to be.

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:43.620
That tends to happen in the middle of blocks
on the periodic table.

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:45.820
But, just because an atom is magnetic,

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:49.240
it doesn’t necessarily mean the material is magnetic.

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:51.820
Don’t ever jump to conclusions in quantum mechanics.

00:05:51.820 --> 00:05:54.980
Getting the loner electrons to line up isn’t enough.

00:05:54.980 --> 00:05:58.220
You also have to get nearby atoms to line
up with each other

00:05:58.220 --> 00:06:01.040
and then get enough regions of atoms to line
up.

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:03.560
We call those regions domains.

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:07.180
The point is magnetic materials are hard to come by.

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:11.360
In fact, there are only four elements that
do this at room temperature:

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:14.960
Iron, Cobalt, Nickel, and Gadolinium.

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:18.140
Beyond that, we either need to get the material really cold

00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:21.660
or build the material using a specially-designed molecule

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:22.800
or both.

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:24.240
So, what the heck is a magnet?

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:28.340
Magnets are what you get when charges move
or at least have momentum.

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:32.660
That’s true if you’re talking about a
single charge, a bunch of charges in a current,

00:06:32.660 --> 00:06:36.720
or even the spin of subatomic charges in a piece of iron.

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:41.200
All magnets come from charges,
so all magnets are electromagnets.

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:42.360
Wicked, huh?

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:44.860
So, how fascinated are you by magnets?

00:06:44.860 --> 00:06:46.180
Let us know in the comments.

00:06:46.180 --> 00:06:47.920
Thanks for liking and sharing this video.

00:06:47.930 --> 00:06:50.250
Don’t forget to subscribe if you’d like
to keep up with us.

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:53.960
And until next time, remember, it’s OK to
be a little crazy.

00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:58.300
In the last video, a big question was:

00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:01.160
Why do the particles have the specific charges that they do?

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:02.560
Well, we don’t know.

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:06.480
Those charges have to be measured and then
put into the standard model.

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:09.340
We haven’t found an underlying mechanism, yet.

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:11.080
Anyway, thanks for watching!

